namespace bo3z::codex
{
	struct Chapter { int book; std::string_view title, image; std::vector<std::string_view> paras; };
	inline constexpr std::array<std::string_view, 5> kBooks{ "The Curse of Alduin", "The Summoner's Path", "The Battle-mage's Path", "The Ranger's Path", "The Spellsword's Path" };  // 0 the lore, 1-4 the class guides (Summoner, Destruction, Ranger, Spellsword)
	inline const std::vector<Chapter> kChapters{
		{ 0, "The Greybeard's Account", "sigil", { "Set down at High Hrothgar by a Greybeard who has broken his silence, for whoever lived through Helgen" } },
		{ 0, "I. Three Words", "helgen", { "Helgen burned, and you lived. But Alduin did not flee: as he rose from the ash he shouted three words into the earth - SLEN TIID VO, Flesh, Time, Undo - the shout that raises the dead, and every barrow in Skyrim answered. His mist rolled over the door of Sovngarde, and now the dead of Skyrim cannot pass to Shor's hall: they lie restless in the ground, and when the sun goes down they claw their way up. The Dragon Priests who swore themselves to Alduin in the Merethic Era remember their oaths and wake with them. Only the Echo Altars the ancient Tongues raised in every hold can still his voice, and they have gone dark. Wake them, city by city, and their dead will sleep again.", "We heard it on the mountain. Not a shout to break stone, but Words sung slow, that sank into the earth and stayed there: <b>Slen</b>, flesh. <b>Tiid</b>, time. <b>Vo</b>, undo. It is the shout by which Alduin raises his fallen kin from their burial mounds, and this time he gave it to the earth itself. By day it sleeps in the ground. When the sun goes down it wakes, and every barrow, every crypt and every churchyard in the holds remembers it.", "Worse: his mist has rolled over the door of <b>Sovngarde</b>. The honoured dead cannot cross to Shor's hall, and a soul with nowhere to go stays with its body. That is why they rise. Free Sovngarde, and the Curse ends." } },
		{ 0, "II. The Dead Who Answer", "graves", { "From dusk until first light the dead climb out of the earth, more each night and harder to kill. At dawn they fall back to dust. The Nords of old had names for them, and the names are true again:", "<b>Haugbui</b>, the mound-dwellers - slow, many, never tired. <b>Pyre-Thralls</b>, the burned dead, still smouldering. <b>Gravegnawers</b>, quick and starved. <b>Barrow Archers</b> on the walls.", "The <b>Tongues</b> - Ash, Rime and Storm - were shouters once; their dead mouths still hold a word or two. <b>Fen-Wailers</b> scream, and the horde comes to the scream: kill them first. <b>Sewn Thralls</b> burst when they fall. <b>Grave-Hounds</b> run in packs, and the <b>Bone Jotunn</b> is the skeleton of a giant, walking.", "Over them all ride the great ones: the <b>Thanes of the Mound</b>, the <b>Mother of Mounds</b>, <b>Haugrisi the Mound-Giant</b>, <b>the Elk-Crowned</b>, <b>Grahzah</b>, Alduin's Grave-Wing, a dragon of bone, and on the Fog Nights <b>Grimmvar</b>, Alduin's Herald, a dragon of shadow - Vulmidviing in his own tongue, 'dark, loyal wings'. Look at a wounded thing and a pale bar shows how much life it has left; the great ones' bars burn gold and bear their names." } },
		{ 0, "III. The Echo Altars", "", { "This is not the first time Alduin has shouted the dead awake. In the Dragon War he did it once over the barrows of the Atmorans, and the Tongues - the first men to learn the Voice - answered him. They raised an altar in every hold where the dead marched: a star of violet fire caught in a ring of floating stones, bound with runes of blue, standing on a floor of black stone inlaid with gold.", "The fire is Alduin's own Thu'um, caught and turned back on itself. The runes are the Tongues' answer, holding it there. Every mage who fell in that war gave their last spell to the star, and the star kept it: that is why the altars have so many to give, and why the holds call them <b>Echo Altars</b>.", "When Alduin was cast out of time the altars went dark and sank into the earth, and towns grew over them. At Helgen he returned, and the altars woke - but only half. Each one has been silenced by one of the <b>Dragon Priests</b> who ruled Skyrim for Alduin in the Merethic Era: their oaths to him outlived them, and the draugr that guard their tombs were their servants in life. When Alduin returned, the oaths woke, and each priest came down from his tomb to the altar nearest it." } },
		{ 0, "IV. Waking an Altar", "wards", { "The altars answer to you. Perhaps it is because you heard the three Words at Helgen and lived; perhaps it is something older. Stand before one and it will set you <b>trials</b> - hold its runic circle while the dead come for you, cull the risen in its streets - and when they are done its Dragon Priest rises to stop you. Strike him down and the altar rings with the Tongues' answer. Its city becomes a sanctuary: no dead rise within its walls.", "There is no Word Wall at an altar. The Tongues cut their words into the altar's <b>heart-stone</b> instead - the great rock at its centre, ringed with runes. Once you have walked the Way of the Voice, stand at a woken altar and the runes give up their words to you, as a Word Wall would: the city's own <b>Thu'um</b>." } },
		{ 0, "V. The Way of the Voice", "", { "Arngeir will tell you, when you have climbed the Seven Thousand Steps, that the altars are older than High Hrothgar. Jurgen Windcaller's Tongues found them after Red Mountain, already ancient, and kept them as he taught: the Voice for worship, not for war. They wrote down what each altar holds - the pages a woken altar gives you, <i>The Broken Words</i>, are theirs.", "Those who stand against the dead with you have their own reasons. The conjurers and battle-mages of the <b>College of Winterhold</b> have seen Morokei walk again; the <b>Dawnguard</b> hunt what rises at night; the <b>Vigilants of Stendarr</b> will not let the dead walk while they draw breath." } },
		{ 0, "VI. The Dragonborn's Road", "", { "The Curse does not replace your own story; it walks beside it. Everything the Greybeards foretold for the Dragonborn still waits for you, and each step of it touches the Long Night.", "<b>Helgen</b> is where it began: Alduin's return, and the three Words he sank into the earth. You came out of it alive, and the road brought you to <b>Riverwood</b>, whose Echo Altar stands nearest the place he rose. It is the first to wake.", "At <b>Whiterun</b> the Jarl will send you to the Western Watchtower, and the first dragon soul you take will show you what you are. Whiterun's altar answers that soul: wake it, and a dragon of your own will carry you.", "When the Greybeards call you up the Seven Thousand Steps to <b>High Hrothgar</b>, walk the <b>Way of the Voice</b>. Without it the altars' heart-stones keep their words; with it, every woken altar teaches you its city's Thu'um. The Greybeards' hall also hides the <b>Reliquary</b>, where the parts the Dragon Priests left behind are forged into wonders.", "The Blades, Alduin's Wall, the Elder Scroll, the truce at High Hrothgar and the hunt for Alduin's lair are the old story, and the Curse does not change them. But Alduin fled to <b>Sovngarde</b>, and his mist on its door is the reason the dead rise. When you follow him there and he falls - the <b>Dragonslayer</b> - the mist thins: the class wonders you have won wake to their <b>Legendary</b> form.", "Even then his three Words lie in the ground. With your class's wonder in hand they wake one by one - <b>SLEN</b>, <b>TIID</b> and <b>VO</b> - each at an old place of the Dragon Cult. Silence all three and the Curse of Alduin is broken for good.", "Those who stand with you have their own roads too: the conjurers and battle-mages of the <b>College of Winterhold</b>, the <b>Dawnguard</b> of Fort Dawnguard, and the <b>Vigilants of Stendarr</b>. Choose your path at an Echo Altar, and your guide will tell you how it grows." } },
		{ 0, "VII. The Voices of the Holds", "", { "<b>Riverwood</b>: <i>Thunder Lash</i> (STRUN BAH QO, 'Storm Wrath Lightning'). Lightning strikes the nearest dead - three, five or eight bolts, each leaping to one more.", "<b>Solitude</b>: <i>Raise the Fallen</i> (DIL VAAZ ZOL, 'Undead Tear Zombie'). The fallen rise to fight for you - two, four or six of them - and burst when their time is up.", "<b>Windhelm</b>: <i>Frost Nova</i> (IIZ SLEN NUS, 'Ice Flesh Statue'). A ring of frost freezes everything around you solid, and the frozen shatter when struck.", "<b>Markarth</b>: <i>Dwemer Quake</i> (GOL VAH DEN, 'Earth Spring Hold'). The ground heaves in front of you and knocks the dead down - with the third word, stone spikes erupt through them.", "<b>Riften</b>: <i>Shadow Step</i> (FEIM ZII GRON, 'Fade Spirit Bind'). You blink forward through the horde, leaving a shadow that draws the dead to it and then bursts.", "<b>Falkreath</b>: <i>Death's Harvest</i> (KRII LUN AUS, 'Kill Leech Suffer'). A draining cone that heals you for every one of the dead it touches - the third word also raises a fallen friend nearby.", "<b>Morthal</b>: <i>Mire Grasp</i> (ZUN HAAL VIIK, 'Weapon Hand Defeat'). Roots burst from the ground where you look, holding the dead in place and poisoning them.", "<b>Dawnstar</b>: <i>Nightmare Howl</i> (FAAS RU MAAR, 'Fear Run Terror'). The dead are thrown back in terror and slowed - and the weakest die of fright.", "<b>Winterhold</b>: <i>Arcane Singularity</i> (SU GRAH DUN, 'Air Battle Grace'). A vortex pulls the dead together where you look, then detonates.", "<b>Whiterun</b>: <i>Dragon's Updraft</i>. A whirlwind lifts you high - and with it, a dragon that will carry you." } },
		{ 0, "Epilogue", "", { "Every Echo Altar in Skyrim is awake. Alduin's voice lingers only in the wild places, and the mist will not lift from Sovngarde's door until the World-Eater falls.", "<i>- written in the silence of High Hrothgar</i>" } },
		{ 1, "The Summoner", "", { "A conjurer of the <b>College of Winterhold</b>. Your summons fight for you - every kill they make is yours: essence, progress and trials.", "<b>You start with</b> Conjure Skeleton (right hand), Conjure Wolf (left hand) and the summoner's robes." } },
		{ 1, "Your Progress", "@progress", {  } },
		{ 1, "Your Altar", "", { "<b>Call a Summon</b> - the first rung of a summon family you don't have. You carry two families at a time.", "<b>Bind a Hero</b> - your Legendary pact: a hero of old (Ysgramor, Miraak, the Ebony Warrior...) or a great army. Opens at <b>six freed altars</b>.", "The five families: <b>the Dead</b> (Skeleton to Death Overlord), <b>Beasts</b> (Wolf to Werebear), <b>Daedra</b> (Dremora to Warlock), <b>Dwemer</b> (Spider to Ballista Master) and <b>the Wild</b> (Spriggan to Earth Mother)." } },
		{ 1, "Getting Stronger", "", { "<b>Kills fill it, the altar raises it.</b> Kills with the spell in your hand count toward its next rung - <b>25</b>, then <b>60</b>, then <b>120</b> for the Legendary rung - once three Echo Altars are freed. Then hold it and take the altar's <b>Reforge</b> row: <b>5,000</b>, <b>8,000</b>, then <b>12,000</b> essence, once a night per spell. The altar's Reforge row and <i>Your Progress</i> show the count.", "<b>Command</b> - how many summons you can have out at once. You start with 1; it grows to 2, 3 and 4 as your two carried families climb (1, 3 and 6 climbs in all). At the limit a new summon replaces the oldest." } },
		{ 1, "Your Perks", "", { "<b>Bonebinder's Brew</b> - summons have 50% more health and hit 50% harder.", "<b>Soul-Tether Wine</b> - each summon kill heals you 5%.", "<b>Conjurer's Command Mead</b> - one more summon at once (up to 5).", "One of each perk a life - death takes them all." } },
		{ 1, "Your Trial and Wonder", "", { "<b>Pact of the Host</b> (your city trial): let your summons make the kills - 10 on Novice up to 30 on Legendary.", "<b>Horn of the Spectral Wyrm</b> (your wonder): after three freed cities, 40 summon kills in each of three cities. It calls a spectral dragon for a minute - until it falls once Alduin is slain." } },
		{ 1, "How a Night Works", "", { "<b>Day</b> (about 20 minutes): the dead sleep. Explore, rest and spend your essence.", "<b>Night</b>: the dead claw up out of the ground (on open ground, never in houses) in <b>waves</b> - fast runners, more and tougher each night, coming from every side. Elites and bosses step out of the Curse's portals. Dawn only comes when the last wave falls.", "<b>Noise</b> draws <b>Volatiles</b> - elite night-runners, far faster and tougher than the rest. Spells bursting, shouts and sprinting all fill the <i>Noise</i> bar under the wave count; let it fill and one comes for you. Go quiet and it drains.", "<b>Essence</b> drops from every kill - spend it at the Echo Altars. Watch for tokens: skull (Insta-Kill), coin (Double Points), falling star (destroys tonight's corpses), chest (Fire Sale).", "Your health, magicka and stamina sit on one plate at the bottom; its rim glows blue while your shout recovers." } },
		{ 1, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Step onto it and activate it. Move with the D-pad or W/S, choose with A or Enter, leave with B.", "From the top: <b>your class</b> (change it for 2,500 essence - each class keeps its own progress), <b>your rolls</b>, <b>Reforge</b> (after three freed altars), <b>perks</b>, then the city's <b>trials</b>.", "<b>Freeing a city</b>: finish four trials (three shared, one your class's), then slay its <b>Dragon Priest</b>, who rises at night. A freed city is a sanctuary, teaches you its Thu'um, and Whiterun gives you a dragon to ride." } },
		{ 1, "Settings", "", { "<b>Difficulty</b> is Skyrim's own (Settings, Gameplay). From Novice to Legendary the dead have <b>0.6x, 0.8x, 1x, 1.6x, 2.2x, 3x</b> health and hit <b>0.5x to 2x</b> as hard. Until three altars are freed, on Adept or harder, no single hit takes more than 60% of a zombie's health.", "Skyrim's own enemies get tougher with the difficulty and with every altar you free, so they keep up with you.", "The <b>Mod Configuration Menu</b> page <i>The Curse of Alduin</i> has the rest: attack and draw speed, field of view, brightness, effects and controls." } },
		{ 2, "The Battle-mage", "", { "A battle-mage of the <b>College of Winterhold</b>: a different element in each hand.", "<b>You start with</b> Scorch (right hand), Hoarfrost (left hand), the Wild Witch's hat and gown and a Destruction tome." } },
		{ 2, "Your Progress", "@progress", {  } },
		{ 2, "Your Altar", "", { "<b>Call a Spell</b> - the first rung of an element you don't have. You carry two at a time.", "<b>Call a Support Spell</b> - Ash Cloud, Cinder Storm, Charged Nova or Symmetry, yours to keep.", "The six elements: <b>Fire</b>, <b>Frost</b>, <b>Shock</b>, <b>Sun</b>, <b>Poison</b> and <b>Blood</b>, four rungs each." } },
		{ 2, "Getting Stronger", "", { "<b>Kills fill it, the altar raises it.</b> Kills with the spell in your hand count toward its next rung - <b>25</b>, then <b>60</b>, then <b>120</b> for the Legendary rung - once three Echo Altars are freed. Then hold it and take the altar's <b>Reforge</b> row: <b>5,000</b>, <b>8,000</b>, then <b>12,000</b> essence, once a night per spell. The altar's Reforge row and <i>Your Progress</i> show the count." } },
		{ 2, "Your Perks", "", { "<b>Ember Heart Ale</b> - Destruction kills burst and hurt the dead nearby.", "<b>Arcanist's Focus Tea</b> - Destruction spells cost 25% less magicka.", "<b>Mana-Siphon Draught</b> - each Destruction kill gives back 6% magicka.", "One of each perk a life - death takes them all." } },
		{ 2, "Your Trial and Wonder", "", { "<b>Trial of the Elements</b> (your city trial): kill with two or more elements - 10 on Novice up to 30 on Legendary.", "<b>Tome of the Six Fires</b> (your wonder): after three freed cities, 40 Destruction kills in each of three cities. One cast looses the top spell of all six elements around you." } },
		{ 2, "How a Night Works", "", { "<b>Day</b> (about 20 minutes): the dead sleep. Explore, rest and spend your essence.", "<b>Night</b>: the dead claw up out of the ground (on open ground, never in houses) in <b>waves</b> - fast runners, more and tougher each night, coming from every side. Elites and bosses step out of the Curse's portals. Dawn only comes when the last wave falls.", "<b>Noise</b> draws <b>Volatiles</b> - elite night-runners, far faster and tougher than the rest. Spells bursting, shouts and sprinting all fill the <i>Noise</i> bar under the wave count; let it fill and one comes for you. Go quiet and it drains.", "<b>Essence</b> drops from every kill - spend it at the Echo Altars. Watch for tokens: skull (Insta-Kill), coin (Double Points), falling star (destroys tonight's corpses), chest (Fire Sale).", "Your health, magicka and stamina sit on one plate at the bottom; its rim glows blue while your shout recovers." } },
		{ 2, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Step onto it and activate it. Move with the D-pad or W/S, choose with A or Enter, leave with B.", "From the top: <b>your class</b> (change it for 2,500 essence - each class keeps its own progress), <b>your rolls</b>, <b>Reforge</b> (after three freed altars), <b>perks</b>, then the city's <b>trials</b>.", "<b>Freeing a city</b>: finish four trials (three shared, one your class's), then slay its <b>Dragon Priest</b>, who rises at night. A freed city is a sanctuary, teaches you its Thu'um, and Whiterun gives you a dragon to ride." } },
		{ 2, "Settings", "", { "<b>Difficulty</b> is Skyrim's own (Settings, Gameplay). From Novice to Legendary the dead have <b>0.6x, 0.8x, 1x, 1.6x, 2.2x, 3x</b> health and hit <b>0.5x to 2x</b> as hard. Until three altars are freed, on Adept or harder, no single hit takes more than 60% of a zombie's health.", "Skyrim's own enemies get tougher with the difficulty and with every altar you free, so they keep up with you.", "The <b>Mod Configuration Menu</b> page <i>The Curse of Alduin</i> has the rest: attack and draw speed, field of view, brightness, effects and controls." } },
		{ 3, "The Ranger", "", { "Sworn to the <b>Dawnguard</b>. Bows and crossbows - every arrow counts.", "<b>You start with</b> an elemental bow, the Dawnguard Crossbow with 30 bolts and the Drifter Outfit." } },
		{ 3, "Your Progress", "@progress", {  } },
		{ 3, "Your Altar", "", { "<b>Call a Bow or Crossbow</b> - one roll: six times in ten an elemental bow, four times in ten a crossbow or offhand bow. You carry one elemental bow; a new one takes its place.", "The bows: <b>Emberstring</b> (fire), <b>Rimehorn</b> (frost), <b>Stormreach</b> (shock), <b>Skyhowl</b> (wind), <b>Dawnspire</b> (sun), <b>Thirstbow</b> (blood). A full draw hits hardest.", "Better crossbows come as cities are freed (Dwarven and Elven at one, Glass and Ebony at two, Daedric and Dragonbone at three). <b>Q</b> / <b>R3</b> at the altar fills your quivers." } },
		{ 3, "Getting Stronger", "", { "<b>Kills fill it, the altar raises it.</b> Kills with a bow count toward its next tier - <b>25</b>, <b>60</b>, then <b>120</b> - once three Echo Altars are freed. Then hold it and take the altar's <b>Reforge</b> row: <b>5,000</b>, <b>8,000</b>, <b>12,000</b> essence, once a night.", "Each tier: more damage, a new attack and a bigger quiver - <b>60</b>, <b>100</b>, <b>150</b>, <b>200</b> arrows - that regrows one arrow every <b>8</b>, <b>6</b>, <b>4</b>, then <b>2</b> seconds.", "<b>Crossbows</b>: kills unlock elemental bolts - fire at 25, ice at 60, shock at 120." } },
		{ 3, "Your Perks", "", { "<b>Fletcher's Sap Draught</b> - your arrows or bolts slowly grow back.", "<b>Eagle-Eye Draught</b> - bows draw and crossbows reload 25% faster.", "<b>Dawnwatch Tonic</b> - with a bow or crossbow out, the dead hurt you 25% less.", "One of each perk a life - death takes them all." } },
		{ 3, "Your Trial and Wonder", "", { "<b>Trial of the Long Shot</b> (your city trial): bow or crossbow kills from 30 m or more - 10 on Novice up to 30 on Legendary.", "<b>The Subsuming Vortex</b> (your wonder): after three freed cities, 40 bow or crossbow kills in each of three cities. Its arrows open black holes that drag the dead in, then burst - wider and stronger once Alduin is slain." } },
		{ 3, "How a Night Works", "", { "<b>Day</b> (about 20 minutes): the dead sleep. Explore, rest and spend your essence.", "<b>Night</b>: the dead claw up out of the ground (on open ground, never in houses) in <b>waves</b> - fast runners, more and tougher each night, coming from every side. Elites and bosses step out of the Curse's portals. Dawn only comes when the last wave falls.", "<b>Noise</b> draws <b>Volatiles</b> - elite night-runners, far faster and tougher than the rest. Spells bursting, shouts and sprinting all fill the <i>Noise</i> bar under the wave count; let it fill and one comes for you. Go quiet and it drains.", "<b>Essence</b> drops from every kill - spend it at the Echo Altars. Watch for tokens: skull (Insta-Kill), coin (Double Points), falling star (destroys tonight's corpses), chest (Fire Sale).", "Your health, magicka and stamina sit on one plate at the bottom; its rim glows blue while your shout recovers." } },
		{ 3, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Step onto it and activate it. Move with the D-pad or W/S, choose with A or Enter, leave with B.", "From the top: <b>your class</b> (change it for 2,500 essence - each class keeps its own progress), <b>your rolls</b>, <b>Reforge</b> (after three freed altars), <b>perks</b>, then the city's <b>trials</b>.", "<b>Freeing a city</b>: finish four trials (three shared, one your class's), then slay its <b>Dragon Priest</b>, who rises at night. A freed city is a sanctuary, teaches you its Thu'um, and Whiterun gives you a dragon to ride." } },
		{ 3, "Settings", "", { "<b>Difficulty</b> is Skyrim's own (Settings, Gameplay). From Novice to Legendary the dead have <b>0.6x, 0.8x, 1x, 1.6x, 2.2x, 3x</b> health and hit <b>0.5x to 2x</b> as hard. Until three altars are freed, on Adept or harder, no single hit takes more than 60% of a zombie's health.", "Skyrim's own enemies get tougher with the difficulty and with every altar you free, so they keep up with you.", "The <b>Mod Configuration Menu</b> page <i>The Curse of Alduin</i> has the rest: attack and draw speed, field of view, brightness, effects and controls." } },
		{ 4, "The Spellsword", "", { "A <b>Vigilant of Stendarr</b>: steel in one hand, a touch spell in the other.", "<b>You start with</b> the Steel Spellsword, a Steel Spellshield, Flame Touch (left hand) and the Practical Pirate outfit." } },
		{ 4, "Your Progress", "@progress", {  } },
		{ 4, "Your Altar", "", { "<b>Call a Spell</b> - the first rung of a touch spell you don't have: Fire, Frost, Shock, Sun, Blood or Poison (three rungs each). You carry two at a time.", "<b>Call a Spell-Weapon</b> - a spellsword, greatsword or spellshield (sometimes a Mysticism shell or cloak). Better steel comes as cities are freed." } },
		{ 4, "Getting Stronger", "", { "<b>Kills fill it, the altar raises it.</b> Melee kills with the spell in your other hand count toward its next rung - <b>25</b>, then <b>60</b> (a power-attack kill counts twice) - once three Echo Altars are freed. Then take the altar's <b>Reforge</b> row: <b>5,000</b> then <b>8,000</b> essence, once a night.", "Each reforge also raises your held spell-weapon a material and unlocks one of four <b>Enchantment Powers</b> (in your Powers)." } },
		{ 4, "Your Perks", "", { "<b>Bladesong Mead</b> - each melee hit heals you 2%.", "<b>Spellbinder's Draught</b> - melee kills give back 10% magicka.", "<b>Steel-Wind Ale</b> - melee swings 20% faster.", "One of each perk a life - death takes them all." } },
		{ 4, "Your Trial and Wonder", "", { "<b>Trial of Steel</b> (your city trial): melee kills with a spell in your other hand - 10 on Novice up to 30 on Legendary.", "<b>Dragon Slayer</b> (your wonder): after three freed cities, 40 melee kills in each of three cities. Every hit carries your off-hand spell; once Alduin is slain, power attacks send it down the line." } },
		{ 4, "How a Night Works", "", { "<b>Day</b> (about 20 minutes): the dead sleep. Explore, rest and spend your essence.", "<b>Night</b>: the dead claw up out of the ground (on open ground, never in houses) in <b>waves</b> - fast runners, more and tougher each night, coming from every side. Elites and bosses step out of the Curse's portals. Dawn only comes when the last wave falls.", "<b>Noise</b> draws <b>Volatiles</b> - elite night-runners, far faster and tougher than the rest. Spells bursting, shouts and sprinting all fill the <i>Noise</i> bar under the wave count; let it fill and one comes for you. Go quiet and it drains.", "<b>Essence</b> drops from every kill - spend it at the Echo Altars. Watch for tokens: skull (Insta-Kill), coin (Double Points), falling star (destroys tonight's corpses), chest (Fire Sale).", "Your health, magicka and stamina sit on one plate at the bottom; its rim glows blue while your shout recovers." } },
		{ 4, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Step onto it and activate it. Move with the D-pad or W/S, choose with A or Enter, leave with B.", "From the top: <b>your class</b> (change it for 2,500 essence - each class keeps its own progress), <b>your rolls</b>, <b>Reforge</b> (after three freed altars), <b>perks</b>, then the city's <b>trials</b>.", "<b>Freeing a city</b>: finish four trials (three shared, one your class's), then slay its <b>Dragon Priest</b>, who rises at night. A freed city is a sanctuary, teaches you its Thu'um, and Whiterun gives you a dragon to ride." } },
		{ 4, "Settings", "", { "<b>Difficulty</b> is Skyrim's own (Settings, Gameplay). From Novice to Legendary the dead have <b>0.6x, 0.8x, 1x, 1.6x, 2.2x, 3x</b> health and hit <b>0.5x to 2x</b> as hard. Until three altars are freed, on Adept or harder, no single hit takes more than 60% of a zombie's health.", "Skyrim's own enemies get tougher with the difficulty and with every altar you free, so they keep up with you.", "The <b>Mod Configuration Menu</b> page <i>The Curse of Alduin</i> has the rest: attack and draw speed, field of view, brightness, effects and controls." } },
	};
}
